=== GLOSSARY ===
UEBA = User and Entity Behavior Analytics
VAE = Variational Autoencoder
GAN = Generative Adversarial Network (here WGAN-GP)
FAR = False Acceptance Rate
TAR = True Acceptance Rate
EER = Equal Error Rate
CI = Confidence Interval (95%)
LOSO = Leave-One-Session-Out
SVM = Support Vector Machine
RF = Random Forest
PE = Policy Engine (classifier standing in for PDP-style allow/deny)
PDP = Policy Decision Point
CLI = Command-Line Interface
CSV = Comma-Separated Values
JSON = JavaScript Object Notation
std = standard deviation

=== RESEARCH_QUESTION ===
Can a Zero Trust-style Policy Engine (PE) trained ONLY on human keystroke
dynamics still reject VAE/GAN-synthesized impersonation samples that mimic an
authorized user’s typing rhythm?

One-liner: Quantify UEBA resilience to generative keystroke impersonation.

=== THREAT_MODEL ===
- Attacker can observe / train on the target user’s enrollment keystroke vectors.
- PE is trained on human genuine + human impostor samples only (never on fakes).
- Attack samples are reserved strictly for testing (open-set style stress test).
- Dataset: CMU Keystroke Dynamics Benchmark (DSL-StrongPasswordData.csv),
  51 subjects × 400 reps of a fixed strong password.

=== PIPELINE_STEPS ===
1. DATA
   DOES: Load/validate CMU CSV from data/raw/DSL-StrongPasswordData.csv
   WHY: Public authentic human baseline matching the dissertation proposal.

2. FEATURES
   DOES: Extract timing feature sets (default hold_flight = Hold H.* + Flight DD.*)
   WHY: UEBA typing fingerprint used by the PE.

3. SPLIT
   DOES: fixed default enroll sessions {1,2,3,4} / verify {5}; optional LOSO
         over sessions {1,2,3,4,5}
   WHY: Train/test separation; LOSO checks session robustness.

4. TRAIN PE
   DOES: Train SVM and/or Random Forest on human enroll + human impostors
   WHY: Stand-in Policy Engine / PDP gatekeeper.

5. ATTACK SYNTHESIS
   DOES: Per-user VAE and/or WGAN-GP trained on enrollment vectors; sample fakes
   WHY: Generative impersonation threat.

6. EVALUATE
   DOES: TAR/FAR/EER at EER threshold; attack FAR at same threshold;
         mean±std±95% CI; Wilcoxon paired FAR tests
   WHY: Report resilience and statistical support for attack > human FAR.

